<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Warmia</h1><p class="st-meta">1243 CE – 1772 CE · capital Lidzbark Warminski</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A church principality in Prussia, ruled by its bishops for over 500 years, where Nicolaus Copernicus worked as a canon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1243 the papal legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-modena">William of Modena</a> divided conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> into four dioceses, one of them Warmia. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> received a third of the diocese as his own territory, while the rest went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>. The bishops made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lidzbark-warminski">Lidzbark</a> their residence and the cathedral chapter settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frombork">Frombork</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> ruled as territorial princes, with their own castles, towns and officials, and Warmia was settled by German colonists alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucas-watzenrode">Lucas Watzenrode</a>, bishop from 1489, brought his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> to serve the chapter, and Copernicus wrote his work on the heavens at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frombork">Frombork</a>. Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaus-hosius">Stanislaus Hosius</a> made it a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> in 1466 Warmia came under the protection of the Polish Crown, keeping wide self-government as part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 gave it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>, which took over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' lands. The last bishop-prince, the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignacy-krasicki">Ignacy Krasicki</a>, kept only his spiritual office.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-warmia.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Warmia" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: J a1 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grunwald_Warmia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1243 CE – 1772 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lidzbark Warminski</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lucas Watzenrode, Stanislaus Hosius, Ignacy Krasicki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Polish, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Poland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ermland, Bishopric of Warmia, Bishopric of Ermland, Warmia</dd></dl></aside></div>

